<InfoLayout
  eyebrow="About"
  title="About PMO Skills"
  description="Nine Claude Agent Skills that encode one PMO's real Notion-and-Slack workflow, from intake through cadence."
>
  <p>
    PMO Skills isn't a product — it's a working set of Claude Agent Skills that encode a project
    management office's actual methodology: how a project's overview gets written, its team
    onboarded, its schedule built, and its Slack and Notion records kept current, week after week.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Each skill started as something a PM was already doing by hand — turning kickoff notes into a
    project page, reconciling meeting notes against an AI transcript, sweeping a channel for links
    that never made it into a workbook. Writing it as a skill just means it happens the same way
    every time, with the PM approving before anything gets written or sent.
  </p>

  <h2>How the table works</h2>
  <p>
    Every row's skill name and description come straight from that skill's own file — not a
    paraphrase written for this site. Stage and Impact are the one layer of editorial judgment
    added on top: which phase of a project a skill belongs to, and how much time it tends to save.
    The source link always points at the real thing.
  </p>

  <h2>Built on</h2>
  <p>
    Notion holds the structured record; Slack is where the actual project conversation happens.
    See <a href="/notion/">Notion</a> and <a href="/slack/">Slack</a> for how each gets used, and
    <a href="/deliverables-first/">Deliverables First</a> for the methodology underneath the
    schedule itself.
  </p>
</InfoLayout>
